(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatology-ii-vignettes"]={"title":"Dermatology II Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 34-year-old man started lamotrigine three weeks ago. He has three days of fever and sore throat, then painful dusky macules on the trunk, erosions of the lips and conjunctivae, and epidermal detachment over about 6% of his body surface. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Erythema multiforme major","Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Toxic epidermal necrolysis","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — detachment under 10% with mucosal involvement, three weeks into an aromatic anticonvulsant. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with papulopustular rosacea has not improved on topical metronidazole after eight weeks. Skin scraping shows a heavy Demodex burden. Which is the most appropriate next treatment?","choices":["Ivermectin 1% cream","Brimonidine 0.33% gel applied each morning","Azelaic acid 15% gel applied twice daily","A high-potency topical corticosteroid applied twice daily"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is superior to metronidazole in Demodex-associated papulopustular disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman with ulcerative colitis has a rapidly enlarging painful ulcer on her shin with an undermined violaceous border. It began as a pustule. A previous surgical debridement made it substantially worse. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Necrotising soft tissue infection of the lower leg","Venous ulceration from chronic venous insufficiency","Squamous cell carcinoma arising in a chronic wound","Pyoderma gangrenosum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the pathergy after debridement is nearly diagnostic, alongside the undermined violaceous border and bowel disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 17-year-old asks whether using a tanning bed before a holiday will protect her from burning. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Tanning beds are classified as Group 1 carcinogens and are not safer than natural sun","A base tan from a tanning bed gives protection equivalent to sunscreen of SPF 15","Tanning beds are safe when used for under ten minutes at a time before travel","Tanning beds emit only ultraviolet A, which does not contribute to skin cancer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the International Agency for Research on Cancer classification is the specific point to make. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man is admitted with Stevens-Johnson syndrome six days after starting trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. Which is the single most important initial action?","choices":["Discontinue the suspected drug and every other non-essential medication","Begin intravenous immunoglobulin at one gram per kilogram for three days","Begin high-dose systemic corticosteroids and continue for several weeks","Apply silver sulfadiazine dressings to the areas of detached epidermis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — earlier withdrawal is strongly associated with improved survival, and each day of delay worsens it. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"A 12-year-old with recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa attends for review. Which surveillance should now begin?","choices":["Annual bone densitometry to monitor for treatment-related osteoporosis","Annual squamous cell carcinoma surveillance, which starts after the age of ten","Annual colonoscopy to detect gastrointestinal involvement of the disease","Annual echocardiography to monitor for the development of cardiomyopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the severe scarring of dystrophic disease carries a substantial carcinoma risk. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has erythema nodosum, bilateral ankle arthritis and hilar lymphadenopathy on chest radiograph. Which is the most likely underlying diagnosis?","choices":["Inflammatory bowel disease, most likely Crohn's disease","Primary tuberculosis with mediastinal involvement","Streptococcal infection with post-infectious reaction","Sarcoidosis presenting as Löfgren syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it has the best prognosis of all sarcoidosis presentations, with over 90% spontaneous remission. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with dermatitis herpetiformis is doing well on dapsone and asks whether she really needs to change her diet. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["A strict lifelong gluten-free diet is the cornerstone and may allow dapsone to be reduced or stopped over one to two years","Dapsone alone controls the condition completely, so a change in diet is optional provided the rash stays quiet","The diet is needed only if she develops gastrointestinal symptoms in the future","A reduced-gluten diet is sufficient provided she avoids obvious sources of wheat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and hidden gluten in medications, sauces and cross-contamination should be discussed. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with primary hyperhidrosis says she has been told repeatedly that she just needs to wash more and relax. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Improved hygiene and stress reduction will resolve the sweating over time","The condition affects a very small minority and treatment options are limited","Sweating of this kind always indicates a serious underlying systemic illness","Primary hyperhidrosis is a medical condition rather than a problem of hygiene or anxiety"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the misconception is addressed directly, and quality-of-life impairment is comparable to severe psoriasis. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has her fourth episode of target lesions on the hands in eighteen months. Each episode has followed an oral herpes outbreak. Which is the most appropriate long-term management?","choices":["Dapsone titrated to response with regular blood count monitoring","Hydroxychloroquine 200 mg twice daily taken continuously","Acyclovir 400 mg five times daily during each acute episode only","Suppressive acyclovir 400 mg twice daily or valacyclovir 500 mg daily"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — recurrent disease is strongly associated with recurrent herpes simplex, and suppression is the answer. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"The parent of a child with a single asymptomatic granuloma annulare ring asks whether treatment is needed. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["It is benign and self-limiting, with about half resolving within two years, so treatment is for cosmesis or discomfort","It is premalignant and should be excised to prevent later transformation","It is contagious to other children and school attendance should be limited","It will persist unchanged for life unless systemic immunosuppressive therapy is started early in the course"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and there is no malignant potential. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has had itchy raised red patches coming and going for three days. Each individual patch fades within a few hours and blanches under pressure, leaving no mark. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute urticaria","Urticarial vasculitis","Erythema multiforme in its early stage","The pre-bullous phase of bullous pemphigoid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — lesions lasting under twenty-four hours and blanching fully is the key diagnostic feature. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman wants treatment for photoaging of her face. Which topical agent is the only one approved by the Food and Drug Administration for this indication?","choices":["Hydroquinone at 2 to 4%","Niacinamide applied twice daily","Tretinoin at 0.025 to 0.1%","Vitamin C at 10 to 20%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it stimulates collagen and inhibits matrix metalloproteinases, over six to twelve months. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman with primary axillary hyperhidrosis has not improved with nightly aluminium chloride. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Microwave thermolysis to destroy the axillary glands permanently","Reassurance that the condition is caused by anxiety and will settle","Glycopyrronium 2.4% cloth applied once daily, or an oral anticholinergic","Endoscopic thoracic sympathectomy to provide permanent relief"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — topical glycopyrronium is approved for axillary disease, with oral anticholinergics as second line. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman spent six hours at the beach. Eight hours later her shoulders are red, hot and tender, without blisters. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Cold water immersion with ice packs applied directly to the erythema","Topical silver sulfadiazine applied twice daily under an occlusive layer","Cool compresses, oral hydration and an early nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug","A short course of oral corticosteroids tapered over the following week"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — early nonsteroidals reduce the prostaglandin-mediated inflammation and pain. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man reports new generalised sweating including at night, with episodes of headache and palpitations, and his blood pressure is 178/104. Which is the most appropriate initial testing?","choices":["Photopatch testing to a standard series of photoallergens","Twenty-four hour urine metanephrines and catecholamines","Minor's starch-iodine test to map the distribution of sweating","Gravimetric measurement of axillary sweat output over five minutes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — episodic sweating with headache, palpitations and hypertension points to phaeochromocytoma. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has target lesions on the palms, and the clinical picture is not clear-cut. A punch biopsy is taken with direct immunofluorescence. Which result would support erythema multiforme?","choices":["Intercellular immunoglobulin G throughout the epidermal layer","Negative direct immunofluorescence with interface dermatitis on histology","Granular immunoglobulin A within the dermal papillae on immunofluorescence","Linear immunoglobulin G along the basement membrane zone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — negativity is what distinguishes it from pemphigoid and pemphigus. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with Stevens-Johnson syndrome reports gritty, painful eyes on the day of admission. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin topical antibiotic eye drops and review in seventy-two hours","Patch both eyes closed until the acute skin disease has settled","Urgent ophthalmology consultation with slit-lamp examination","Reassure and reassess the eyes once the skin has re-epithelialised"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — ocular involvement drives the blinding sequelae of symblepharon and corneal scarring. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man reports months of intensely itchy, burning bumps on his elbows, knees and buttocks. Examination shows grouped papules and excoriations symmetrically distributed, with few intact vesicles. He has no gastrointestinal complaints. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Linear immunoglobulin A bullous dermatosis","Dermatitis herpetiformis","Scabies infestation of the trunk and extremities","Atopic dermatitis of adult onset"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the symmetric extensor distribution with burning that precedes lesions, and gastrointestinal symptoms may be absent despite villous atrophy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man returns from a beach holiday with painful blisters followed by odd streaked brown marks on both forearms. He had been squeezing limes into drinks in the sun. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Berloque dermatitis from bergapten in a fragrance product","Chronic actinic dermatitis of the forearms and dorsal hands","Allergic contact dermatitis to an ingredient in his sunscreen","Phytophotodermatitis from furanocoumarins in lime juice"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — linear streaked hyperpigmentation after lime plus sun is the classic presentation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 72-year-old outdoor worker has rough, scaly, erythematous papules with a sandpaper texture scattered across his balding scalp and dorsal hands. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrheic keratosis","Bowen disease","Scalp psoriasis","Actinic keratosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — sandpaper texture on chronically sun-exposed skin, the commonest precancerous lesion worldwide. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 15-year-old with a body mass index in the 97th percentile has velvety, darkened, thickened skin at the back of the neck and in both axillae. Which is the most appropriate initial testing?","choices":["Skin biopsy of the posterior neck for histological confirmation","Fasting glucose, haemoglobin A1c, fasting insulin and a lipid panel","Computed tomography of chest, abdomen and pelvis with upper endoscopy","Potassium hydroxide preparation of scrapings from the affected skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the finding is a visible marker of insulin resistance and warrants metabolic assessment. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman develops itchy 3 mm red papules on her chest and forearms hours after the first sunny weekend of spring. The same thing happened last April and settled by midsummer. Her face is spared. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Solar urticaria of the chest and forearms","Erythropoietic protoporphyria of adult onset","Polymorphous light eruption","Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — spring onset, sparing of chronically exposed skin, and summer hardening are all characteristic. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman is recovering from carbamazepine-induced Stevens-Johnson syndrome and is being discharged. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Avoid sun exposure and use broad-spectrum sunscreen daily to prevent the eruption from recurring","Avoid the causative drug class for life, wear a medical alert bracelet, and have first-degree relatives counselled on shared genetic risk","Avoid the causative drug for six months, after which a cautious supervised rechallenge in hospital is reasonable to consider","Expect complete recovery within weeks, with no need for any specialist follow-up after discharge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the human leukocyte antigen association makes family counselling part of the discharge advice. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has an itchy, eczematous eruption on sun-exposed skin that extends onto covered areas, beginning after two weeks of a new sunscreen. Which test establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Antinuclear antibody panel including anti-Ro and anti-La antibodies","Photopatch testing with duplicate sets, one of which is irradiated","Minimal erythema dose testing to ultraviolet A and ultraviolet B","Skin biopsy of the eruption sent for direct immunofluorescence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is the gold standard for photoallergy; a reaction only on the irradiated set confirms it. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman newly diagnosed with rosacea asks whether treatment will cure it. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Avoiding alcohol alone will resolve the condition within several months","Laser treatment removes the underlying disease and prevents any recurrence","It is a chronic condition that therapy controls rather than eliminates, so long-term management and daily sun protection are expected","A twelve-week course of oral antibiotics usually produces a permanent remission with no need for any further treatment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside a trigger diary and gentle non-irritating skincare. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with toxic epidermal necrolysis has been admitted to the burn unit and the offending drug stopped. Which pharmacologic therapy has the strongest current evidence base?","choices":["Pulsed methylprednisolone continued for fourteen days","Subcutaneous omalizumab given every four weeks","Cyclosporine at 3 to 5 mg/kg daily, started early","Prophylactic broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — early initiation is preferred and the evidence is the strongest of the available options. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has one facial pigmented macule that is asymmetric with an irregular border and two darker areas within it, unlike her other lesions. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Biopsy the lesion to exclude lentigo maligna","Treat the lesion with cryotherapy for five seconds","Begin topical hydroquinone 4% and review in three months","Reassure and arrange routine review in twelve months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — atypical or uncertain morphology on dermoscopy is the stated indication for biopsy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with rosacea reports gritty, red eyes and blurred vision that has worsened over two weeks. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin topical ivermectin to the face and review in six weeks","Begin sub-antimicrobial doxycycline and review in three months","Advise artificial tears and reassess at his next routine appointment","Urgent ophthalmology referral for slit-lamp examination"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — ocular rosacea with visual symptoms risks corneal scarring if untreated. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has had daily wheals for four months despite a standard dose of cetirizine. She is otherwise well. Which is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Increase the non-sedating antihistamine to up to four times the standard dose","Begin omalizumab at 300 mg subcutaneously every four weeks","Begin cyclosporine with monitoring of blood pressure and renal function","Prescribe a five-day course of prednisone at 40 to 60 mg daily"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — scheduled up-dosing is the next step before adding other agents. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 39"}]};
